<section id="A1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.6 </span>Alignment and Contrastive Training Data</h3>

<div id="A1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The sources and statistics of the data used for our Sparse Alignment Pretraining are reported in Table <a href="#A1.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ A.6 Alignment and Contrastive Training Data ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Milco: Learned Sparse Retrieval AcrossLanguages via a Multilingual Connector" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. In total, the corpus consists of 594 million bi-text pairs, each containing one English sentence and one non-English sentence with the same semantic meaning.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The statistics of the data used for our contrastive training are shown in Table <a href="#A1.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ A.6 Alignment and Contrastive Training Data ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Milco: Learned Sparse Retrieval AcrossLanguages via a Multilingual Connector" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>. This dataset contains 1.4M queries collected from 16 datasets, covering English, Chinese, and 16 additional languages from Mr.TYDI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and MIRACL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., b</a>)</cite>. Similar to prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lei et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our training data also includes many in-domain datasets from BEIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
